#import "../lib.typ": *

#section(
  exercise([
    #drill-heading(65)

    In this drill, capital G is made first to a count of three, and then,
    without lifting the pen, followed with six revolutions of the reverse
    traced oval. Count as follows: 1, 2-3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9.

    #instruction[Write seventeen exercises in 60 seconds.]
  ], lines: 6, examples: ("drill-65", "drill-65-2")),
  exercise([
    #drill-heading(66)

    Study the form of capital G closely before attempting to make it; be sure
    that you not only know when you make a poor letter, but that you know why
    it is poor. One bad stroke may spoil an otherwise good letter. Learn to
    locate the bad strokes. Count 1, 2, 3; or 1, stop, 3, for each letter.

    #instruction[Write forty to fifty G's in 60 seconds.]
  ], lines: 6, slots: 2, examples: ("drill-66", "drill-66-2")),
  exercise([
    #drill-heading(67)

    This is a good business form. Special instruction is unnecessary. Use your
    eyes.

    #instruction[Write fifty G's in 60 seconds.]
  ], lines: 6, examples: ("drill-67", "drill-67-2")),
  exercise([
    #drill-heading(68)

    The angular finishing stroke gives a connective line for any letter that
    may follow, and admits of joining the capitals for an extended drill as
    well. Count 1, 2, for each letter, or 1-2, 3-4, 5-6, for each group of
    three.

    #instruction[Write eighteen groups of G's in 60 seconds. Three to a group.]
  ], lines: 6, slots: 3, examples: ("drill-68", "drill-68-2")),
  exercise([
    #drill-heading(69)

    Nothing can be better at this stage of the work than easy words constantly
    repeated. Through a series of repetitions, strength in movement is
    developed, and faults are seen.

    #instruction[Write "Gunning" twelve to fourteen times in 60 seconds.]
  ], lines: 6, slots: 4, examples: ("drill-69", "drill-69-2")),
)
